From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris McLaughlin <19711592+qBitnaut@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 13:17:22 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] SL7 local: arm64: dts: qcom: x1e80100-romulus: Clock the
 front camera from 12 MHz

The OV02C10 of the Surface Laptop 7 runs from 12 MHz, not 19.2 MHz: with
the 19.2 MHz driver settings the front camera streams at 18.8 fps
(30 * 12 / 19.2 = 18.75) and lowering the frame length to 1462 lines
(vertical_blanking=370) mid-stream gave 29 to 30 fps. This matches the
linux-media report by Ryan Thomas Cragun (2026-07-02) that the machine
drives the OV02C10 from a fixed 12 MHz clock. The Windows camera resources
for MSHW0470 list no MCLK, so the module most likely carries its own
oscillator, and the camera clock controller cannot generate 12 MHz.

Model the clock as a 12 MHz fixed clock referenced by the sensor, and drop
the CAMCC MCLK4 reference and its 19.2 MHz assigned rate. The pinctrl
state with the gpio100 MCLK pin is kept: it only selects the pin function
and the sensor does not depend on a clock on that pin.

Declare the 250 MHz link frequency the driver now requires with a 12 MHz
clock (500 Mb/s per lane, 100 MHz pixel rate), which is also what CAMSS
reads from the sensor's link frequency control to compute the CSIPHY
settle count.

Upstream-Status: Pending (depends on the ov02c10 12 MHz support patch)
---
 .../dts/qcom/x1e80100-microsoft-romulus.dtsi    | 17 +++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/x1e80100-microsoft-romulus.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/x1e80100-microsoft-romulus.dtsi
index d26817e92d0a..12827c0bffec 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/x1e80100-microsoft-romulus.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/x1e80100-microsoft-romulus.dtsi
@@ -62,6 +62,17 @@ backlight: backlight {
 		pinctrl-names = "default";
 	};

+	/*
+	 * The front camera module has its own 12 MHz oscillator: the Windows
+	 * resources for MSHW0470 list no MCLK and the sensor runs from 12 MHz
+	 * whatever CAMCC MCLK4 is set to, which cannot make 12 MHz anyway.
+	 */
+	ov02c10_xvclk: clock-12000000 {
+		compatible = "fixed-clock";
+		#clock-cells = <0>;
+		clock-frequency = <12000000>;
+	};
+
 	gpio-keys {
 		compatible = "gpio-keys";

@@ -944,9 +955,7 @@ camera@36 {
 		pinctrl-names = "default";
 		pinctrl-0 = <&cam_rgb_default>;

-		clocks = <&camcc CAM_CC_MCLK4_CLK>;
-		assigned-clocks = <&camcc CAM_CC_MCLK4_CLK>;
-		assigned-clock-rates = <19200000>;
+		clocks = <&ov02c10_xvclk>;

 		orientation = <0>; /* front facing */

@@ -957,7 +966,7 @@ camera@36 {
 		port {
 			ov02c10_ep: endpoint {
 				data-lanes = <1 2>;
-				link-frequencies = /bits/ 64 <400000000>;
+				link-frequencies = /bits/ 64 <250000000>;
 				remote-endpoint = <&csiphy4_in_ep>;
 			};
 		};
